+// Wrap a global tap cell into [0, grid_size): the grid is periodic, exactly
+// as ducc0's dump() writes taps modulo (nu, nv). Taps stay within one support
+// of the grid, so a single conditional add/subtract suffices.
+inline int wrapTap(int cell, int grid_size) {
+  if (cell < 0) return cell + grid_size;
+  if (cell >= grid_size) return cell - grid_size;
+  return cell;
+}
+
+// Split grid coordinates: the visibility buffer holds (base, frac) per axis,
+// split on the CPU in double precision. base is the exact integer floor cell,
+// frac in [0, 1) the sub-cell position with full 24-bit precision. Storing the
+// coordinate as one float32 quantises the fraction to ulp(cell), which
+// corrupts kernel interpolation for long baselines (measured 3e-04 on real
+// data). Never form base + frac in float32: that sum re-quantises the
+// fraction it was split to protect. Anchor from the parts instead:
+//   floor-anchored (poly family): i0 = int(base) + centre, f = frac.
+//   round-anchored: nearest = int(base) + (frac >= 0.5), f = frac - (frac >=
+//     0.5 ? 1 : 0), giving the [-0.5, 0.5) offset exactly.
